                <pb facs="0312" n="302" sameAs="frus:frus1937v05/pg302"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">824.6363 ST 2/186: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Bolivia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">La
                                Paz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-25T17:00:00-04:00">October 25, 1937—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>62. Referring to my despatch 53, dated October 23 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >22</hi>], Calvo has now been reliably informed that the Government is
                    considering a decree in few days requiring submission of controversy to court
                    within 90 days. Such action would automatically prevent direct discussions which
                    Calvo now agrees with Metzger in recommending to New York. Foreign Office
                    requests copies of messages contained in Department’s telegram No. 10, of April
                    26, 8 p.m. and 14, May 7, 1 p.m. and of Finot’s reply which Finot did not leave
                    in their files. In the circumstances the copies requested or fresh instructions
                    of similar tenor seem desirable to promote direct friendly negotiations.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caldwell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
